#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d496f36-d9ee-5b26-b44c-c1968857daf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d496f36-d9ee-5b26-b44c-c1968857daf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256434} :answer-field/id #uuid "baff0e2d-9b61-5780-9004-7951a0b3c029" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d37a6373-85f7-5021-93bf-bde115d91e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100235" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(-1)^{4}+4)^{2}}{(-1-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4271776-2565-508b-a4c0-05856a0d5c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4271776-2565-508b-a4c0-05856a0d5c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256437} :answer-field/id #uuid "32124313-f85d-5185-9fc8-c729515e34da" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91317a16-9fb5-5d43-9550-4a0b2404efdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-100236" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3) & =({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2)^{3})\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2))}^{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & =((-2)^{3}+2)^{3}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2)+3) \\\\  & =(-6)^{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7f0189-8bdb-58cf-8aaa-952657ad5d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7f0189-8bdb-58cf-8aaa-952657ad5d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256440} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92060c6-8853-5b65-a4a6-b4eb8d49cf80" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "855a5e48-e137-5598-94f7-f4832740f0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-100264" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}({x}^{2}-3x-5))}^{5}}-\\sqrt{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}(2x-7))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{({4}^{2}-3(4)-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2(4)-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(16-12-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(8-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1}-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6a2bcd-3314-5ee1-8472-b7a09d44d3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a2bcd-3314-5ee1-8472-b7a09d44d3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256446} :answer-field/id #uuid "99476705-d581-5b54-a13c-b34652061ee2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "beb0d5cc-39b2-586c-83f5-8cf543207207" :question/math-academy-id "q-100275" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{3}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-8+4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-2(2))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-4)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e708382f-3705-58a5-9a18-333922210b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e708382f-3705-58a5-9a18-333922210b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256449} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df85a12-1e10-5d7f-ad90-818a82b74222" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256448}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d37402e0-45e5-5794-8d62-3744bcf93a6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100277" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-3(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(3(2)-4(1))^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =-32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm With a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53710983-408a-5a6c-90ea-b34ff38359ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53710983-408a-5a6c-90ea-b34ff38359ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256482} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da1e781-a4dc-50ed-92d1-c932089c7436" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256481}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70c8405e-c25e-581b-907c-1bd10f59fc85" :question/math-academy-id "q-100674" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{15}(10)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{15}(10)$ by changing the base $15$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=15,$ $X=10,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{15}(10) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(10)}{{log}_{10}(15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(10)}{\\operatorname{log}(15)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1}{1.176\\,091} \\\\  & \\approx 0.850\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm With a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28c51bc3-a49c-557b-86c0-c22af227fbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28c51bc3-a49c-557b-86c0-c22af227fbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256485} :answer-field/id #uuid "722b0dfa-4a0b-5cc5-9de6-df174301443b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256484}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2f720ea-4efd-5c38-87f6-787d4de5f50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-100675" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(30)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(30)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=30,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(30) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(30)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(30)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.477\\,12}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.64\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm With a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a98f76-933b-551d-9eab-cfe391420e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.969" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a98f76-933b-551d-9eab-cfe391420e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.969" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256488} :answer-field/id #uuid "601db05c-084b-5144-a803-3fc02ba6ccb5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2c3dd1-20e9-5368-9ec4-088bb43cb692" :question/math-academy-id "q-100676" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{22}(20)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{22}(20)$ by changing the base $22$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=22,$ $X=20,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{22}(20) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(20)}{{log}_{10}(22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(20)}{\\operatorname{log}(22)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.301\\,030}{1.342\\,423} \\\\  & \\approx 0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change Given a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92f610ab-f996-522b-87af-a4586c3c9d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f610ab-f996-522b-87af-a4586c3c9d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256537} :answer-field/id #uuid "accec121-d526-5711-937f-a3434f20da60" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9ca4c60-8351-5109-8b21-60704b041116" :question/math-academy-id "q-101730" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ is $-1,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4p+1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,p+6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(-5)}{5-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+6)-(4p+1)}{5+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3p}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=-1$ on the interval $[-5,5].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3p}{10}=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-3p}{10} & =-1 \\\\ 5-3p & =-10 \\\\ -3p & =-10-5 \\\\ -3p & =-15 \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d47bf5-d562-56c1-981f-81ce1bfb0996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d47bf5-d562-56c1-981f-81ce1bfb0996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256610} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a58f9f1-7f33-5634-9a0d-d31653864f38" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc04cfe-5c1c-58c0-b534-2ea8f4897bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-103216" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-2$ and ${a}_{2}=0,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =0+(-2) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffa1a3f-0c84-5504-a5b3-3da85ca9f1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ffa1a3f-0c84-5504-a5b3-3da85ca9f1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c87d266-0ee7-554e-9da5-6db1aebe77e7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d61383ab-6ca0-5c21-90fc-da34d0ca7d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-103217" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=4$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4+5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbb57b6c-f1e6-5978-b142-a098b7bb0037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbb57b6c-f1e6-5978-b142-a098b7bb0037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616} :answer-field/id #uuid "309ffe6e-1a0d-5db1-8e6e-7d5c01c645e2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0798bf8d-4438-5b94-a6a0-6a186fe4b314" :question/math-academy-id "q-103222" :question/problem "What is the fourth term of the following sequence?\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=-2\\,,\\,f(2)=0,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =0+(-2) \\\\ f(3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =-2+0 \\\\ f(4) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7149c441-adba-55e5-97a8-21d1a37322d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7149c441-adba-55e5-97a8-21d1a37322d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256619} :answer-field/id #uuid "0531825e-15d9-55ac-8251-aacc8bb9e54a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256618}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22ea45b9-f009-53ac-920f-698b600ba579" :question/math-academy-id "q-103223" :question/problem "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=-1$ and $f(2)=1,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =1+(-1) \\\\ f(3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Y-Intercept of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29957c6e-c46a-5da1-952f-b90fda674c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29957c6e-c46a-5da1-952f-b90fda674c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a39660-f172-58c9-b080-c4ef45aa5957" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79f2ac8f-706d-5c75-bc5d-c89a9e2fab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm Without a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bae5cc-a920-50b7-932f-de5a6e7134cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6bae5cc-a920-50b7-932f-de5a6e7134cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256762} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f15f019-2ba2-5997-922b-a7d174fc823e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256761}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4051d6e3-232c-59f8-9d4f-58de8b478ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108374" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{125}(625)$ without using a calculator.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=125,$ and $X=625.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}(625) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(625)}{{log}_{5}(125)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{4})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210cc233-6585-5a6d-b06c-9bc1bdb493a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210cc233-6585-5a6d-b06c-9bc1bdb493a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256797} :answer-field/id #uuid "772e26ca-ee00-5682-8c86-cb49edb6fb71" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "399c8dfe-cb86-5d6a-b44f-74bba4001d09" :question/math-academy-id "q-110080" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9cd032667b7138c5456cf9fa1a1cc954f1c8003779ac54fa58f6bbad296db4a1.png)\n\nWhat systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $2x+y=1.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-2x+1,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n2x+y>1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies  *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>1 \\\\ y<x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e18d451-4c7d-5649-9a73-0b00fa5d26e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Negative Square Root of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "563fb3fe-f33f-5523-a72f-de46890b952a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "563fb3fe-f33f-5523-a72f-de46890b952a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256850} :answer-field/id #uuid "01cbce41-64f7-5c3b-9548-cb2aa37324c1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a7660b-b110-5991-98aa-28a0f141dba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-111721" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-15}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-15} & =-\\sqrt{15\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{15}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90028ddc-fde1-5c0c-8653-8e93f56c4057" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Number With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447005c9-6710-5f95-becc-6f02a7e746d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "447005c9-6710-5f95-becc-6f02a7e746d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256853} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf0aa97-1b4c-50f1-ae63-1d2eb35230a6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b18cb4ef-a56d-5053-8e77-e1968dc8458c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111741" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{-180}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nSince $180=36\\cdot 5,$ the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-180} & =\\sqrt{180\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Powers of i Using the Laws of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "663a2306-738c-57ca-bff9-92357aaf260b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "663a2306-738c-57ca-bff9-92357aaf260b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256908} :answer-field/id #uuid "17777363-aed8-56fb-b2c6-70ad72085ac5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbdb58f0-667e-5aa7-8bff-41bad7b32ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-113033" :question/problem "Simplify ${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}=(1)^{26}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Negative Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc8407b-d6ef-533c-b4f4-5ab84427489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc8407b-d6ef-533c-b4f4-5ab84427489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256911} :answer-field/id #uuid "456339f4-a0d9-5d0b-a8d0-490f373da9dc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92f2f0b7-de4f-58b0-acaa-26e31d056305" :question/math-academy-id "q-113087" :question/problem "$9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{4}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}=\\frac{9}{1}=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eac8cd01-c52e-5e9c-80fc-114b81d2daa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257095} {:answer/id #uuid "bcee4bb5-984a-5ee2-a65d-e06c31f0a27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257096} {:answer/id #uuid "1b153650-8e00-5782-898a-033beb61c782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257097} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e465-e283-57f1-a505-024b3e1f71fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257098} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d59aa3-7f16-5213-b7b6-2f801122b419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257099} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab33868-c654-53bf-a8df-b79b7cd623cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257100} {:answer/id #uuid "b90288af-b97b-5a19-9f87-e5687fa1c355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257101} {:answer/id #uuid "db2f86aa-b780-5dcb-ba7e-cb54eaab2841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b153650-8e00-5782-898a-033beb61c782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257097} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdd74f5-16f9-550f-8f1e-4eb6c3e50781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6afc5777-c42f-5d0a-931f-1320237e4acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114605" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7x+4}=-9$\nII. - $\\sqrt{3x-2}=3$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{9x-5}=-2$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3916e240-fb7f-535e-887a-c9e1c5a5d840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257105} {:answer/id #uuid "33d66366-35e9-5641-878c-f406fa2cff58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257106} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5ccb7b-514f-53e2-8cda-6932d842e9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257107} {:answer/id #uuid "d56b2ee6-b94d-53ec-9172-b66232466e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257108} {:answer/id #uuid "0829c6b4-2de6-5d00-bbff-b771f8714cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257109} {:answer/id #uuid "4fdeeb94-db0d-5f3f-8e17-5f724464727a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257110} {:answer/id #uuid "47659802-aae3-56c1-b33e-41edb3963bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257111} {:answer/id #uuid "0eacfd5e-10a2-54d0-b2b3-050e45056245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fdeeb94-db0d-5f3f-8e17-5f724464727a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257110} :answer-field/id #uuid "c061fa44-2283-5b2d-9a3a-45d58533becb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55431f3f-1e50-5d2e-ae53-dc462ee5ce94" :question/math-academy-id "q-114606" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7-2x}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{x-3}=-7$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{6x-2}=5$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2404a8fb-8cf7-5292-8507-88750527799b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2404a8fb-8cf7-5292-8507-88750527799b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257409} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8f1de7-1eee-58e2-b14a-6b1290a784d1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257408}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02046115-abbf-5f23-b026-4fef673ef48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120211" :question/problem "Some drinks of different sizes and flavors were sold during a school party. The two-way frequency table below summarizes the sold drinks.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected sold drink is a small Fanta.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected drink is a small Fanta, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{50}=0.16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4500f0e5-fb34-5b1e-9aa7-f00bfd7572a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4500f0e5-fb34-5b1e-9aa7-f00bfd7572a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f6fb75-fc6d-5913-93b2-5261fe6c7f00" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a26858a-19aa-5552-b214-8bb2060beda5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120875" :question/problem "Researchers surveyed students in School A on which superpower they would most like to have. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nSchool B has $300$ students. Assuming the distribution of results is the same for both schools, estimate the number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the number of students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points at the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{28}{100}=0.28.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected School A student is a male and chooses to fly as his superpower.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we multiply the total number of School B students $(300)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =300\\cdot p \\\\  & =300\\cdot 0.28 \\\\  & =84\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ce6f026-571d-5730-a17f-97642ece7d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce6f026-571d-5730-a17f-97642ece7d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257834} :answer-field/id #uuid "c623e0f0-67a4-5542-ad44-fac37c1b8f0c" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57fdd752-b18e-5774-9009-153f8666ff8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fdd752-b18e-5774-9009-153f8666ff8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257836} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28b87fc-978b-5edd-9ffa-5aa40ef27c4a" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2810621-4a9f-5666-b439-842c3d34a484" :question/math-academy-id "q-131072" :question/problem "Solve $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}$ by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $2$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v+12 & =16 \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v-4 & =0 \\\\ (3v+2)(v-2) & =0 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n- For $v=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-4(-\\frac{2}{3})+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $v=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3({2}^{2})-4(2)+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{12-8+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0$ are $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1495831c-3fef-5728-be92-747772130cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1495831c-3fef-5728-be92-747772130cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257901} :answer-field/id #uuid "3edf62ed-a7f4-5c27-aa57-65f9839c59ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4dc0446-bdab-566c-a1ea-d739465a89a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133092" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9875c946-efae-5e29-b405-94de8fc3b770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9875c946-efae-5e29-b405-94de8fc3b770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257904} :answer-field/id #uuid "d72cf376-ab9d-5294-aa38-2dbf171dad01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4facbf96-c4c8-5f38-a25b-4b60a9204fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-133093" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>3,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x-5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d522a8-ae3c-5723-909e-540ddac1d9da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de2c845-8e92-58ae-94f4-adea8d283336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de2c845-8e92-58ae-94f4-adea8d283336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257907} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3867252-3213-5383-8a8b-466b65ee7411" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dac5550f-7f7a-5ad7-9089-a64688784e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133094" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583857a8-0144-5997-9ea1-7321299750da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "583857a8-0144-5997-9ea1-7321299750da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257910} :answer-field/id #uuid "4123edc7-00ac-585c-92fc-0df39a14aa4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d26441c-ac3b-59d3-b427-084701de0ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133095" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f76f7203f1ba17f8148bbc1140420034a631f46e6995a3bda5453ec3fd9a6f55.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8acee5-720e-5a47-8fb9-ed105708b61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8acee5-720e-5a47-8fb9-ed105708b61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d22b92-cbdd-578f-962c-a6f0554738bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "722596b3-9815-59b7-8c7b-48c557696a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133096" :question/problem "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x+1})+(3x-2) \\\\  & =3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd0a4d20-c266-5180-8d8c-aa495464977f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a4d20-c266-5180-8d8c-aa495464977f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7eefcc-e5e0-599e-a5d6-7a86d1ced9d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94dd89e7-71bf-5758-b5b6-2bb250a0931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133097" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ h(4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f954ff1-3f02-5fb1-aca2-5911d5636167" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec85d45-705f-58f2-9ecb-beeb96887d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec85d45-705f-58f2-9ecb-beeb96887d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba2c0da-5e9e-5cee-afa1-b64d8b64f6c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db7e3081-13ac-5bac-97f6-3cdfeabac772" :question/math-academy-id "q-133098" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=3.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(3) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b93ae47e-c9a4-5c74-b810-01aab25b220c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b93ae47e-c9a4-5c74-b810-01aab25b220c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} :answer-field/id #uuid "9082cc46-6619-50c2-b109-27b601c47f11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a2bc2f-fe61-5929-96b2-b7033bdcf072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a2bc2f-fe61-5929-96b2-b7033bdcf072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257924} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d698e12-f6f5-591d-a04a-2546aa9ce49f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a61a79f3-a45a-5593-b4bc-eb0ce92364fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-133099" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47eb01a66773e5cf2b223cc3f8f4ff02caa424170a8e090ec0b1e45ed6d69741.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607fe3ff-c70e-5e90-8be3-b217f42489ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607fe3ff-c70e-5e90-8be3-b217f42489ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257927} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06794a5-596c-5119-8f53-ec6c5b564819" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c61acd1-ade2-5a44-8b2b-c8e5c8ab07c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133100" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-2$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-2)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a535cd-451a-5972-81b1-3fd08d261865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a535cd-451a-5972-81b1-3fd08d261865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258023} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9c33e1-f33c-57e4-8739-951e96637668" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807e93c4-5bb4-5de5-8fbc-1aec915f7b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-13585" :question/problem "If $f(x)=x+4$ and $g(x)=-3x+2,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(x+4)-(-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+3x)+(4-2) \\\\  & =4x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdded031-fd95-5a7f-92d4-c0d6713ba64d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb92eab9-fe3e-51ee-9863-781454b2fb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb92eab9-fe3e-51ee-9863-781454b2fb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258113} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c56db0-c487-5ad8-8d42-607d7b438555" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6b0a5be-d880-5387-a7aa-c743ad533f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-14152" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{3}-(-\\sqrt{3}))}^{2}+{(5\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16(3)+9(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a95e18a-ab4f-581f-bed2-a542bf0c822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a95e18a-ab4f-581f-bed2-a542bf0c822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258116} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f5cc2f-a333-5375-b493-ddb51c74c61f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258115}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec2a46d4-91b6-5d7c-83d9-c6d69abedfb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141615" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-12x+28.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbd1f6f-6266-508a-9271-24f3d4543ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbd1f6f-6266-508a-9271-24f3d4543ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7063e60-d887-53de-8f03-68e4e7251e17" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258717}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f7fded4-a7f4-5dbc-a96e-2d5c3e995127" :question/math-academy-id "q-17280" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change Given a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8877ffb7-f58c-5fbd-ba52-99c8f52a63f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8877ffb7-f58c-5fbd-ba52-99c8f52a63f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258780} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53d7482-1cba-564a-9af7-5083dcff0367" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f11677ac-9262-56f2-8cbc-9d4cbc75e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11677ac-9262-56f2-8cbc-9d4cbc75e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258782} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f890d1d-8aea-54ba-95ff-94ffdb406cff" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "149a5bb5-23b4-56cd-ac8a-edc3c7f35e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-19154" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,23]$ is $1.5,$ find the value of $p.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ | $\\,21\\,$ | $\\,23\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-{p}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,-13\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ |\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(23)-f(1)}{23-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-{p}^{2}-4)}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=1.5$ on the interval $[1,23].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}=1.5.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22} & =1.5 \\\\ {p}^{2}+17 & =33 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =33-17 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =16 \\\\ p & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-4$ or $p=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change Given a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7415d098-c067-5d3a-a6ae-5cd2e605e7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7415d098-c067-5d3a-a6ae-5cd2e605e7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258785} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3ef0df-5c83-5dcc-979e-1d960c4c3f11" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258784}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e3ab4e9-a63e-5231-88df-c45b46948041" :question/math-academy-id "q-19160" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-11\\,$ | $\\,2p-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,p+10\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-2)}{3-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+10)-(2p-1)}{3+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-p}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[-2,3].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{11-p}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11-p}{5} & =3 \\\\ 11-p & =15 \\\\ -p & =15-11 \\\\ -p & =4 \\\\ p & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68635d27-a9ef-541b-a57b-d9f5f422ae85" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a978ec35-daa3-5475-b451-48874291889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a978ec35-daa3-5475-b451-48874291889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258841} :answer-field/id #uuid "d69339cc-4899-5ceb-bc91-5dc45b77c967" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258840}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2e31816-43ec-559b-a472-2ed0efb972fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-20823" :question/problem "A curve is defined as $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$  Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[2({4}^{3})-{4}^{2}+5(4)]-[2({1}^{3})-{1}^{2}+5(1)]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{132-6}{3} \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dab1977-579e-50a4-86c6-15428cba7f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dab1977-579e-50a4-86c6-15428cba7f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258847} :answer-field/id #uuid "d381769d-59ee-54e7-87b7-fa734ad44d15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95d04696-3038-5931-a2a7-b332a1053193" :question/math-academy-id "q-21123" :question/problem "The number of machines infected by a computer virus is represented by the function $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus was first detected. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11$ be the number of machines infected by the computer virus after $t$ days. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,7]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(0)}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[4(7)^{3}-2(7)^{2}+5(7)+11]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}+5(0)+11]}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1309}{7} \\\\  & =187\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first week is $187$ machines per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b073db-b437-5dc9-988f-2b5987716d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b073db-b437-5dc9-988f-2b5987716d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6e5463-ac4d-5d4f-a5c3-955e6ac78200" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d77c7163-8f4b-52b0-9277-4b1a28b029a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-218682" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of a city, measured in thousands of people, $t$ years after $2012,$ is given by the expression $P(t)=5{t}^{2}+t.$\n\nThe average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$ was\n{{field-1}}\nthousand people." :question/worked-solution "Let $P(t)=5{t}^{2}+t$ be the population of the city, in thousands of people, $t$ years after $2012.$ Since $2020$ is $8$ years after $2012$, to find the average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,8]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(8)-f(0)}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5({8}^{2})+8)-(5({0}^{2})+0)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{328-0}{8} \\\\  & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, The average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$ was $41$ thousand people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87ee4eae-702f-59f0-aff4-46c9acb89f6c" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Logarithm Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de5aa4ab-4544-59c2-b200-37613f4e1558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de5aa4ab-4544-59c2-b200-37613f4e1558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258951} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaecb80d-b491-5cb5-a4f4-54d654e1a717" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c1dd647-0f9b-5884-88c2-d3741fd6e4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-23197" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{k},$ what is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=3$ to base $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}x & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3} & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}({9}^{1/2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68635d27-a9ef-541b-a57b-d9f5f422ae85" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7a199b2-1930-5e1a-a031-920f36d45057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a199b2-1930-5e1a-a031-920f36d45057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ef738b-16f7-55c6-9245-458eb8fb9ec6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259000}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2abca8-7e9d-54bd-8cdb-f6670388afba" :question/math-academy-id "q-2432" :question/problem "A curve is defined as $y=3{x}^{2}-7.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[2,5].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-7.$ Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(2)}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3(5)^{2}-7]-[3(2)^{2}-7]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{68-5}{3} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa081d4-f85a-5239-b034-b78894429611" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61dadb9c-cc86-5b9c-90e9-dd71a729ccee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61dadb9c-cc86-5b9c-90e9-dd71a729ccee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259012} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc1d6bc0-9357-50f3-a715-df23fe79ae2d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259011}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ba2473a-7860-526f-a636-2580b5f71073" :question/math-academy-id "q-24635" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2c688a8451bcdf9a0fcc23f0dea2355c66c73f6114ef07f48535d9dd3e5eeb12.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real roots\"." :question/worked-solution "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the parabola does not have any roots."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50506808-de9a-51bb-9395-185ea71aaaad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50506808-de9a-51bb-9395-185ea71aaaad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259383} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d701d3-71f6-53e4-bd4d-224633ad5700" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b6e17eb-b41f-58d1-bd2b-5852d16cb44e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3043" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2.$\n\n$a$: {{a}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{2a+4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 2a+4 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 8+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{16+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $a=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2$  is $a=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4030a2b3-34aa-5d00-bd95-e382ec781b20" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression by Factoring Second-Order Polynomials in Both the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd4fdb9-b196-55f1-a817-3649db551250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2k+6}{k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd4fdb9-b196-55f1-a817-3649db551250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2k+6}{k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260088} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b4d8c4-f1f6-57ef-88e2-e3209f3ca8bb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260087}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fb4fa9e-d01e-54da-a47b-046997e1a366" :question/math-academy-id "q-3489" :question/problem "$\\frac{2{k}^{2}-18}{{k}^{2}-6k+9}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{k}^{2}-18}{{k}^{2}-6k+9} & =\\frac{2({k}^{2}-9)}{{k}^{2}-6k+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(k+3)(k-3)}{(k-3)(k-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(k+3)\\cancel{(k-3)}}{(k-3)\\cancel{(k-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(k+3)}{k-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k+6}{k-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db5e330d-2cdf-5ad1-9ff4-ab4c886ff4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5e330d-2cdf-5ad1-9ff4-ab4c886ff4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260165} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e1331a0-8fbd-524a-8c07-5d64e2c40536" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbe7843b-5fc3-5be1-af00-dea6856d2e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-35252" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2} & ={lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to 1}(2{y}^{2}+1))}^{3}\\cdot {({lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2{(1)}^{2}+1)}^{3}{(4(1)-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{3}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =243\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc01e6e-6ce6-5bc8-b813-3f42cc3847c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc01e6e-6ce6-5bc8-b813-3f42cc3847c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260174} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d3983f-d0e2-5d03-9246-2bc06892aa79" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "238f1bf7-e926-5a9a-b458-b3d344bdf40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35295" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2404cbf-3ca7-59ae-a93b-18a00631f502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2404cbf-3ca7-59ae-a93b-18a00631f502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260183} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9111b4-8506-5726-8c62-7d58cfb11fa0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18d96d23-85b4-508f-bbdc-a059435bb5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-35621" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e647ebf6e8332e9be5abd75e0f5ac5102c89c4a40590cef21a8ddfc65a17701d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}={lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3(-1)^{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(2-1)}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb1e753f-ed91-5ba4-bd25-5fba48060d58" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Cubes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5b4ae7-d446-565d-8840-2a1c4124b6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(x+2y)({x}^{2}-2xy+4{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b4ae7-d446-565d-8840-2a1c4124b6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(x+2y)({x}^{2}-2xy+4{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260219} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10cbec4-2c07-5a22-947a-31490dc19a99" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260218}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f555f4c-a2a8-5de1-aa66-3ef3e108a7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-37556" :question/problem "Factor the expression $8{x}^{3}+64{y}^{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $8.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $8,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n8{x}^{3}+64{y}^{3}=8({x}^{3}+8{y}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a sum of cubes:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{3}+8{y}^{3})=8({x}^{3}+(2y)^{3})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression in parentheses using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+64{y}^{3} & =8({x}^{3}+(2y)^{3}) \\\\  & =8(x+2y)({x}^{2}-x\\cdot 2y+(2y)^{2}) \\\\  & =8(x+2y)({x}^{2}-2xy+4{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf4dce6-1e7d-5176-950f-96b4537b78af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf4dce6-1e7d-5176-950f-96b4537b78af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260273} :answer-field/id #uuid "d059153d-357b-56ea-b351-d096c5692bff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260272}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "193e9c3b-5823-5fe7-b42e-9a9b3e4a9d8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39825" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8x-5} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8x-5})}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ 8x-5 & =3 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8\\cdot 1-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{8-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}$  is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0798a66-db69-5497-b608-6cd7176099fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0798a66-db69-5497-b608-6cd7176099fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260276} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2b07a8-a82f-5b65-88a0-840cdbabae14" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0845673e-0a7d-58b4-9254-65ae15f4f4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39830" :question/problem "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef03f08c-e294-5878-842e-eb5f397b1825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef03f08c-e294-5878-842e-eb5f397b1825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260279} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8d9b98-e0a3-59ab-aa53-d00a41de0596" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260278} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4e387d-4612-5b07-aae3-26f3b30882c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4e387d-4612-5b07-aae3-26f3b30882c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260281} :answer-field/id #uuid "736a8b92-b3e8-56c3-8089-3512e8c5dd0c" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260280}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98003bca-fa53-57e1-a8e6-79179173f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39832" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-4 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-(-4)-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16+4-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=-4,\\,5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4$  are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3e1d694-1bdd-5948-8c96-890ceae62640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e1d694-1bdd-5948-8c96-890ceae62640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260284} :answer-field/id #uuid "700ead04-7a16-50e8-975b-a572438d1758" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99449027-c0b9-5dd4-9166-86aaa4b8715c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99449027-c0b9-5dd4-9166-86aaa4b8715c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260286} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad8d745-e329-584d-8a11-ce5b919ce507" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260285}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0f5b567-86e0-5c3b-94d7-e63615575a3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39833" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37} & =5 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37})}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+37 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}-8\\cdot 2+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-16+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-8\\cdot 6+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{36-48+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=2,\\,6$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5$  are $x=2$ and $x=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7475235a-62e7-5fae-98a6-1664adf39916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7475235a-62e7-5fae-98a6-1664adf39916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260350} :answer-field/id #uuid "1891fb5a-ae3c-53e5-bcfb-2e2ebdc3a191" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "894785c9-b37d-573f-b85e-093f2abc4eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41478" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/34ba4a4dcd6655d68fbc20fb6192354ae755cc7eaf14a2459e9b7012ffe6d9ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $4,$ what is the value of $f(62)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(62) & =f(62-4) \\\\  & =f(62-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(62-15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9a8eb1-1589-50a7-aec1-2595510c8f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9a8eb1-1589-50a7-aec1-2595510c8f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260411} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d213a5a-61a1-56dc-a89c-7e909aa9753d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f232a13-ac59-5c7f-b9ee-ff0c688c0095" :question/math-academy-id "q-4460" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+3(1)+4}+6(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+3+4}+6 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+6 \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2) & =4(1)-2=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2} & =\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae0f137c-f1e6-5c07-842d-8713cd771e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f137c-f1e6-5c07-842d-8713cd771e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260440} :answer-field/id #uuid "d24908d8-a2f5-5436-aecb-56c6458ab874" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0371bfd-8501-506c-b9fa-24609b84ea8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-46814" :question/problem "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c16e723f-f101-552c-b2b0-a44b2c6fdb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16e723f-f101-552c-b2b0-a44b2c6fdb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260469} :answer-field/id #uuid "743e6ede-3996-50f5-bcb2-0cea97ae3446" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ed5c80c-e475-58de-a971-282836200277" :question/math-academy-id "q-47139" :question/problem "The employees and the interns of a company were asked whether they commute to work by car or by bus. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nLet $I$ be the event that a randomly selected person is a **\"Intern\"**, and $B$ be the event that a randomly selected person commutes to work by **\"Bus\"**. Estimate $P(I\\cup B).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $I\\cup B$ is the event that a randomly selected person is an intern or person who commutes to work by bus. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $I\\cup B,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(I\\cup B)=\\frac{16+14+26}{80}=\\frac{56}{80}=0.70.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554bd0ac-14d9-5d7e-b6fa-9a9fbd6f80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554bd0ac-14d9-5d7e-b6fa-9a9fbd6f80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260475} :answer-field/id #uuid "df64faa5-9c44-5a00-8262-d22fb90135ed" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75eedbd1-62f9-545d-b2b8-ed88a19bd586" :question/math-academy-id "q-47299" :question/problem "Rose surveyed $168$ children in Arizona High School. She asked them about their favorite topics in math, between geometry and algebra. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below. \n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected child is a **\"Boy\"** and $G$ the event that a randomly selected child likes **\"Geometry\"**. Estimate $P(B\\cap G).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $B\\cap G$ is the event that a randomly selected child is a boy and likes geometry. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap G,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(168),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap G)=\\frac{56}{168}=0.333.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddcdc7a1-98ce-5f26-a400-e2c7ecdb290f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "203" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddcdc7a1-98ce-5f26-a400-e2c7ecdb290f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "203" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260478} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ca5f3b-ab39-53ed-8854-3d49571cdace" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "146b2ef3-9c02-5d19-b5a0-7ac41d8998b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47306" :question/problem "A group of $5$th grade and $6$th grade students at Benjamin Franklin Elementary School are randomly selected for a survey. They are asked about their favorite places to go on summer vacations between the beach and the countryside. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nJohn Adams Elementary School has $250$ students in $5$th grade and $6$th grade. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both elementary schools, estimate the number of students at John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations (or both). Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the number of students who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"$6$th grade\"** or the column **\"Beach\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(160),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{55+42+33}{160}=\\frac{130}{160}=0.81.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is in $6$th grade or likes to go to the beach on summer vacations.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(250)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =250\\cdot p \\\\  & =250\\cdot 0.81 \\\\  & =202.5 \\\\  & =203,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Powers of i Using the Laws of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da397cf0-577d-5fc2-a133-0ee5fec95c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da397cf0-577d-5fc2-a133-0ee5fec95c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260754} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df60c92-dcf7-5f84-9b2b-e78a9ed3f90b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260753}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e22a5106-9b86-5df9-82bd-ae96786f3991" :question/math-academy-id "q-538" :question/problem "${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=(1)^{2015}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e8ad55-058a-5d71-ac05-3f8533f8bd46" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Small Positive Integer Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6549fd-c3ae-50ba-a921-f0da391c0f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6549fd-c3ae-50ba-a921-f0da391c0f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260766} :answer-field/id #uuid "81455e6a-1e14-5bc5-83bd-0eef65be8ca5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260765}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "229ffe7b-501b-5f8e-8a23-2f28dfd64018" :question/math-academy-id "q-546" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\text{i}}^{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that ${\\text{i}}^{3}=-\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "760a1a5b-4c06-547d-9248-a6acb97c4b84" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Positive Integer Power Using the Cyclic Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b721c5a8-f447-5d0a-8137-886105d797b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b721c5a8-f447-5d0a-8137-886105d797b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260774} :answer-field/id #uuid "690025c3-3162-54f0-9482-c6813f64a78f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8437f94-1d26-5f61-9246-7a0a4a80faa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-549" :question/problem "${\\text{i}}^{14}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=14.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n14÷4=3\\,\\text{R}2.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{14}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ecd9c9-41c0-58a2-840f-deec00020b6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40519f37-cbd5-57a8-838b-c1d3330cdaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40519f37-cbd5-57a8-838b-c1d3330cdaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260777} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cf63c4-461e-590f-b47c-292436c38438" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6501a8-c1cf-5be1-848d-9970c0779f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-56" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{-9}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-9} & =\\sqrt{9\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ecd9c9-41c0-58a2-840f-deec00020b6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1ed1f0a-dc2e-51e1-8893-15c29b747a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ed1f0a-dc2e-51e1-8893-15c29b747a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb2f41e-7435-5ec1-aaad-140347f5d156" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a63fca-c627-59bf-af30-ba385e73b94c" :question/math-academy-id "q-61" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{-13}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-13} & =\\sqrt{13\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e18d451-4c7d-5649-9a73-0b00fa5d26e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Negative Square Root of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0565b4-43e1-5be4-9e08-3175ab4dec0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b0565b4-43e1-5be4-9e08-3175ab4dec0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260901} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d52415-2bf2-5897-a5d5-0dead8539396" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd15e777-ccda-5d2f-8e60-26ac4b1a571b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66222" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-36}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-36} & =-\\sqrt{36\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e18d451-4c7d-5649-9a73-0b00fa5d26e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Negative Square Root of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7174f19e-2b2b-5d94-ae67-b9d5aa4b4ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7174f19e-2b2b-5d94-ae67-b9d5aa4b4ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260904} :answer-field/id #uuid "744da57f-b06f-5e5d-a908-87d5916c6382" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "311cb5ae-915d-58e4-ba9d-9ef92c48315b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66223" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-25}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-25} & =-\\sqrt{25\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90028ddc-fde1-5c0c-8653-8e93f56c4057" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Number With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da56c139-c398-5b9e-8c09-a7f84db87e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da56c139-c398-5b9e-8c09-a7f84db87e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260910} :answer-field/id #uuid "703f5ba3-59ec-5cb4-96ad-1175b103312b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cbde6cf-0e45-5c99-bf6a-3df156d4e49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66226" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-8}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nSince $8=4\\cdot 2,$ the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-8} & =-\\sqrt{8\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020697e9-c2fc-57d1-9fc9-5bb1c13a6fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e4f3cc-2370-598b-87d1-c1eebc198ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e4f3cc-2370-598b-87d1-c1eebc198ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260913} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e71a553-1ce1-5a59-84b1-1c2013ff890b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ebbdd24-e97c-5ef9-8692-f3145c68ea3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66235" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-\\frac{16}{9}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-\\frac{16}{9}} & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020697e9-c2fc-57d1-9fc9-5bb1c13a6fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240737e7-8e13-5104-a6fa-735cfd096f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "240737e7-8e13-5104-a6fa-735cfd096f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260916} :answer-field/id #uuid "6763b942-ee0c-5a78-a981-a1ac7258580b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be123228-4149-57f4-a7e3-eae84dca8b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-66236" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{49}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{49}} & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{49}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{49}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{49}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e8ad55-058a-5d71-ac05-3f8533f8bd46" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Small Positive Integer Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e681740f-7a97-5e98-ac7a-808fbbb2af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e681740f-7a97-5e98-ac7a-808fbbb2af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260924} :answer-field/id #uuid "d958c418-00f9-566e-b673-2785517e3e74" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68b8235-f32a-5c69-b523-5c1357944070" :question/math-academy-id "q-66385" :question/problem "What is the value of $4{\\text{i}}^{3}-5{\\text{i}}^{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{3}-5{\\text{i}}^{2}=4(-\\text{i})-5(-1)=5-4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Negative Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593fe36f-a7fe-5072-a247-39f4491b9f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "593fe36f-a7fe-5072-a247-39f4491b9f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260945} :answer-field/id #uuid "a58bb529-ef27-5657-bf19-9e025da5b449" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ba253e5-6b77-5991-9dca-b8648ddf6d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66490" :question/problem "$-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=-\\frac{4}{(-1)}=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm Without a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03a8b08-8e7b-5cd4-a133-f21c8ad23ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03a8b08-8e7b-5cd4-a133-f21c8ad23ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260992} :answer-field/id #uuid "c060282b-a228-5149-88ee-93425f941ef2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260991}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf175bf7-41d9-5d9c-9ca1-8254b6c5a5b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-66880" :question/problem "${log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=25$ and $X=\\sqrt{125}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125}) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{125})}{{log}_{5}(25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{{5}^{3}})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{3/2})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm Without a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37b4e09-7c5d-59e7-869c-54c3b277f653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a37b4e09-7c5d-59e7-869c-54c3b277f653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260995} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e6003b-62b3-5c4a-a6fd-c807d1c3bba4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260994}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47a63a18-20ff-5e2a-89aa-c14176c04a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-66887" :question/problem "${log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $X=\\sqrt{27}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27}) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{27})}{{log}_{3}(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{{3}^{3}})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}({3}^{3/2})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm With a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa06c276-2514-578f-8d2c-d26527f7e6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa06c276-2514-578f-8d2c-d26527f7e6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261009} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f59870-2611-597e-8a30-5301563263a2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e980299-bf4e-5e95-b34b-d63085c5feea" :question/math-academy-id "q-66934" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(35)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(35)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=35,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(35) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(35)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(35)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.544\\,07}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.71\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87ee4eae-702f-59f0-aff4-46c9acb89f6c" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Logarithm Using the Change of Base Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b7b539-5b9e-5c98-b5c1-1ecbb853e83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61b7b539-5b9e-5c98-b5c1-1ecbb853e83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261021} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec665f11-4ef7-53b9-87bb-a43387bde5a7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261020}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8865266-a05d-5ca3-b448-4ff1c8f96fa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-67158" :question/problem "Give an expression equivalent to ${log}_{81}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=81$ to base $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{81}x & =\\frac{{log}_{3}x}{{log}_{3}(81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}x}{{log}_{3}(81)} & =\\frac{{log}_{3}x}{{log}_{3}({3}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{81}x=\\frac{{log}_{3}x}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6c0abe-be01-5a18-89bf-81ac56e02c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6c0abe-be01-5a18-89bf-81ac56e02c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261078} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7242dff-1d2d-5dcb-a2ec-c3039ce685d0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93e1f776-dbc1-58f3-a68d-2ac0bb33599b" :question/math-academy-id "q-67696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2)^{4}}{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2))}^{4}}{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1-2)^{4}}{(-1+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{1} \\\\  & =81\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f0457c-6fea-5192-9240-e27267ee21eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f0457c-6fea-5192-9240-e27267ee21eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f6064a8-9b36-5d48-95f7-99018aa0905b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261080}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e345d3a4-c2d8-581c-8529-3152719d2521" :question/math-academy-id "q-67699" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/90f51ea4561fc1c1b892863daedc15bb813fbf18a03e1c9366d54f1a883d0db4.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4}={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (\\pi /2))^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2))}^{4} \\\\  & =(1-2)^{4} \\\\  & =(-1)^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(f(x)-2)^{4} \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2})\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249be7b5-1906-53fd-b666-f188a9dbdb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "249be7b5-1906-53fd-b666-f188a9dbdb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261087} :answer-field/id #uuid "369ba6c0-530d-5f3a-85c4-0d0286705012" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f14e9302-b81e-5bd4-b3e0-dc1fe5b82277" :question/math-academy-id "q-67702" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1786cfa3ea3b08a97e59ec148a8fe710d6e4b11fc1cbc3582c4bf925a41bf19f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(17-2f(x))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17-2\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "051b5337-7671-599f-a7f3-c3fb064575cc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Interval Over Which the Average Rate of Change is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5504da93-d9a6-5500-b49b-8ddb0bacf15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5504da93-d9a6-5500-b49b-8ddb0bacf15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261102} :answer-field/id #uuid "992e25e8-25ee-56cb-bbbd-521f7d1ca04a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261101}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4551be98-ab92-5187-a58d-9f84d3f63f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-69673" :question/problem "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(3)=f(7)=-2,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[3,7].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "051b5337-7671-599f-a7f3-c3fb064575cc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Interval Over Which the Average Rate of Change is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41820006-8575-577b-8637-c873853c0bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41820006-8575-577b-8637-c873853c0bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261105} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a093ec-b1ec-5c51-b524-05e9791e7039" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "855a5a4d-d544-5179-beb7-e1ec84d7bfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-69674" :question/problem "Find an interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-1)=f(9)=-4,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-1,9].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea8573f-d2ec-5460-9d3a-4dd595f8b002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5z-3w}{4z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea8573f-d2ec-5460-9d3a-4dd595f8b002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5z-3w}{4z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154} :answer-field/id #uuid "4320af23-4440-5115-9980-b996e937f94c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ec7633e-93ac-5273-b67e-f7908232a0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-70143" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{2z-5w}{4z}+\\frac{3z+2w}{4z}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $4z,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-5w}{4z}+\\frac{3z+2w}{4z} & = \\\\ \\frac{2z-5w+3z+2w}{4z} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2z+3z)+(-5w+2w)}{4z} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z-3w}{4z}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99746b9a-db42-5c76-8f6b-77a4de7baadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261266} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb5cd38-555a-5de4-ad48-126e9197b058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261267} {:answer/id #uuid "c143786c-c752-5d2e-9903-0c52b74f138f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261268} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd1718f-44b0-551a-bbe4-cbdf43e9b0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261269} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad651d-436f-570b-bf14-69598cf6d8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261270} {:answer/id #uuid "3b03393d-0fc3-5bfe-bedd-0ae250e58214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261271} {:answer/id #uuid "386e3c4b-41c6-5019-8f5b-b033d14e311d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261272} {:answer/id #uuid "4651d533-ecbc-522b-9a54-12bd4e20021e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b03393d-0fc3-5bfe-bedd-0ae250e58214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261271} :answer-field/id #uuid "db82101f-7c0e-501d-90c5-0ffe2bb275d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261265}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd9d77ea-492f-56f5-8abf-5300e4f7832a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70907" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{3x+1}=-1$\nII. - $-\\sqrt{4-x}=6$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2x+1}=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b547e8bd-ee5a-56dc-abe0-5665aab273f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b547e8bd-ee5a-56dc-abe0-5665aab273f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3b6073-83d3-5a4b-be0c-891adaa49651" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624dc58c-08a0-55d9-80aa-dc7d9874467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "624dc58c-08a0-55d9-80aa-dc7d9874467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261288} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf043e1-fb2b-52ea-83c7-e40ff55f2741" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261287}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90b1010c-b5bd-524a-86d1-a7ed6794e479" :question/math-academy-id "q-70913" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+6 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-3(-2)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+6+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-3(5)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-15+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe0d77e-52ec-55af-86ee-7865dc316245" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Monomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad687d2-f64e-5027-8c29-e8305e93f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ad687d2-f64e-5027-8c29-e8305e93f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261613} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a00dc30-5eb1-5d80-ac45-2d5ccaa66e8e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb1d235-c690-5225-8779-e89c17dee989" :question/math-academy-id "q-76311" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $6w,$ $3{w}^{3},$ and $4{w}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three monomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable parts. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,12,18,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,6,9,12,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,8,12,16,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $w,\\,{w}^{3},$ and ${w}^{2},$ we raise $w$ to the largest power that appears in all the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,{w}^{3},{w}^{2})={w}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6w,3{w}^{3},4{w}^{2})=12\\cdot {w}^{3}=12{w}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052bd356-c7b0-5129-9bcb-82306acddb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "052bd356-c7b0-5129-9bcb-82306acddb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261654} :answer-field/id #uuid "288f7944-c565-5e24-8c60-31ea17ec42df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261653}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ee25c9-f495-51c7-80ce-fd000c80af30" :question/math-academy-id "q-77740" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1)}{{lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1) & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}-1 \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x-2) & =3(4)-2 \\\\  & =10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2} & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e8864d-bc03-575e-be49-c7cf32935637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e8864d-bc03-575e-be49-c7cf32935637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261657} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdd126b-7cfa-5038-8832-9a861d6fa162" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261656}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "336e3700-4bb5-5eae-b287-47106b8a9236" :question/math-academy-id "q-77743" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/44196cce68dab39addc447b332273371d5becb94bc0068e5200eeda596239ffa.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=-2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3)=2(-2)+3=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4)=-2+4=2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{8}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3a77654-921b-5889-8e20-20ce6a10e20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a77654-921b-5889-8e20-20ce6a10e20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261660} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97970c3-1cbe-5e91-83e2-dc25e22d9a12" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261659}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fd05b19-c616-5951-a797-4ee3e4c55001" :question/math-academy-id "q-77745" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4b3ef4ce6c3c2d40c60639b8467649155fb7e8a3c018dff359bf766a86cf8054.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1)=3(-1)-1=-4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1)=-1-1=-2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{(-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f719f4d7-cf22-5f8a-8dfc-d38a20455eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f719f4d7-cf22-5f8a-8dfc-d38a20455eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261687} :answer-field/id #uuid "788a116e-9747-58b0-969a-b50e6fe4712d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6b1c009-5d14-58f7-8b31-aa7d55ec544b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78108" :question/problem "The number of bacteria in culture is given by the function $f(t)=-0.6{t}^{2}+10t+50,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since the bacteria was isolated. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first $10$ minutes?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=-0.6{t}^{2}+10t+50$ be the number of bacteria in the culture after $t$ minutes. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first $10$ minutes, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,10]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(0)}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-0.6(10)^{2}+10(10)+50]-[-0.6(0)^{2}+10(0)+50]}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{90-50}{10} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first $10$ minutes is $4$ bacteria per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee485a03-1c8b-5057-919b-1f3f20f95696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee485a03-1c8b-5057-919b-1f3f20f95696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261709} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e3ceb3-20be-5e8a-9228-255334c275ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261708}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f233d26-a151-550e-abff-b0d56859a52f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78618" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=10-{x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=5-2x,$ find  $(f+g)(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =10-(2)^{3}=2 \\\\ g(2) & =5-2(2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(2)=f(2)+g(2)=2+1=3\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(10-{x}^{3})+(5-2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(2) & =-(2)^{3}-2(2)+15=-8-4+15=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffe05e10-59ca-5e25-968e-10a0fd41d964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261715} {:answer/id #uuid "3bac9522-9348-55e2-8330-c263d930694f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261716} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ebc174-5cc1-5840-9e5c-5604061f4908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261717} {:answer/id #uuid "8d215e9d-5ee0-531c-9c56-59b308a6aae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261718} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e5aee1-5d41-56e7-b00c-80ee9183cb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261719} {:answer/id #uuid "4c181833-3588-536c-81c1-ced79756a50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261720} {:answer/id #uuid "677dc537-57f0-5a09-8d3d-50b6e22a18b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261721} {:answer/id #uuid "986b5eb8-60ce-5158-9a08-c5ea42ea71a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d215e9d-5ee0-531c-9c56-59b308a6aae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261718} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f576e7a-0943-56d5-9e53-5531538e2081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e48707eb-c35d-5d97-8abc-4a048269c15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79248" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{2x-4}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{8-3x}=1$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{x-7}=4$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I states that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equation I has no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2230ade-0920-5220-9045-cd6d1c6a1d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261725} {:answer/id #uuid "126cee81-9a83-5954-83a2-22c702f4a5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261726} {:answer/id #uuid "57308651-1460-57b4-a7d1-30b9fe7b42ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261727} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4c9c70-57f5-5518-a8ed-9b7f71ff2227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261728} {:answer/id #uuid "00f59731-286b-54c8-86e4-a1b98f41bf30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261729} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd7cc04-afa2-5f31-b312-8df61565bf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261730} {:answer/id #uuid "b700074f-ff81-5931-a4ec-44982358a62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261731} {:answer/id #uuid "15950900-69d0-5101-973e-12dc011fb487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57308651-1460-57b4-a7d1-30b9fe7b42ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261727} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2b5810-9eef-5df4-8922-1d1b9118af75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261724}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1727d27-de04-53c4-a9de-9b0d34a8d70c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79249" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{3x+7}=-4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{5x-6}=2$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{4x-3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e836ca22-abd9-5812-bbf7-3b057e3d6c4b" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0582ae-d8b4-5c86-b73f-5193dd36a49d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{s}^{2}-4)(2{s}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd0582ae-d8b4-5c86-b73f-5193dd36a49d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{s}^{2}-4)(2{s}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261741} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9d22de-45d5-583e-b977-a8735bcadb92" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d893db-0a35-530c-94cc-02031aeec566" :question/math-academy-id "q-79497" :question/problem "Factor $6{s}^{4}-11{s}^{2}+4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $t={s}^{2}$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{s}^{4}-11{s}^{2}+4 & =6{({s}^{2})}^{2}-11({s}^{2})+4 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-11t+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-11t+4 & =(3t-4)(2t-1) \\\\  & =(3{s}^{2}-4)(2{s}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5ea0012-4792-591a-9de7-6bbb67cc1e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ea0012-4792-591a-9de7-6bbb67cc1e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261747} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa6f51a-4684-5e94-8b70-ee3994a3c1cb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c0f2338-2ab2-574c-954b-5e423f2531e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79540" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $2x,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{x-3+3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3+3)+(x+2x)}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance Given the Number, Sum, and Sum of Squares of the Data Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-87562" :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8effb25-abe6-5e37-8f2c-91ad12868b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Stretching a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58d8976a-8d6a-571f-aa3c-4dfb863d3bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d8976a-8d6a-571f-aa3c-4dfb863d3bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4523332-86c1-57a4-af85-58822a8ad619" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcfc92c-4860-5df6-b3a0-3c09ec63b05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fcfc92c-4860-5df6-b3a0-3c09ec63b05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261948} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a41c4b-0097-56b8-a60f-6552b9d773bb" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76d5aa8d-6b7f-5a59-a437-5e21bbd91bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-88205" :question/problem "A stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis is applied to the point $(5,-6).$  What is the resulting point?\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying a stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-\\frac{1}{2}y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is\n\n$$\n(5,-6)↦(5,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(5,3).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/76787875dff29fa140881c6de2ee0d7c08baf394994e31246204d21bbcf61f56.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c98ce69-8410-51a4-8d9c-eec7b46fb83b" :knowledge-point/title "Global vs. Local Extrema"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab74f05-3afc-5968-87f4-be4d044e94c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261963} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b21ac8-297f-5559-8890-905d5525ed71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261964} {:answer/id #uuid "2de2d434-323b-5bd0-88c3-4a9168bb5e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261965} {:answer/id #uuid "e35d3b70-777c-576e-9426-bd095fa9b141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261966} {:answer/id #uuid "8f387637-8b17-53e2-9637-872e8c44aabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} {:answer/id #uuid "8b88f3ab-2919-5f84-8aa0-b48c86a90793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261968} {:answer/id #uuid "57b09224-30af-51ef-b156-ee01bb809d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261969} {:answer/id #uuid "6be2d733-af51-531c-a6ae-fb3b8f9f6ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f387637-8b17-53e2-9637-872e8c44aabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261967} :answer-field/id #uuid "1396d91b-da49-5297-9871-ffd0d3227bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261962}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "029317bc-fcf6-501c-9125-ecc74c217368" :question/math-academy-id "q-90623" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/632da466eb26480490a49f9c5a71e0d6f95653efa749a8f117d81e016fd969ae.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\nII. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=3$\nIII. - $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(1)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=3.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(3).$ For example, $f(6)<f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "760a1a5b-4c06-547d-9248-a6acb97c4b84" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Positive Integer Power Using the Cyclic Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f4302b-9b17-599a-a78a-d26ae23b33b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f4302b-9b17-599a-a78a-d26ae23b33b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261976} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff4dbc0-536a-591e-8ea0-df48c5f297f8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4b2df1e-6d40-59ba-a881-5e43b1cc0734" :question/math-academy-id "q-924" :question/problem "${\\text{i}}^{33}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=33.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n33÷4=8\\,\\text{R}1.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{33}={\\text{i}}^{1}=\\text{i}.\n$$"}]}
